>2026-03-27</div>\n   260→        </div>\n   261→    </header>\n   262→\n   263→    <div class=\"nav-bar\">\n   264→        <a href=\"#new-products\">New Products</a>\n   265→        <a href=\"#whitelist-updates\">Whitelist Updates</a>\n   266→        <a href=\"#technical-insights\">Technical Insights</a>\n   267→    </div>\n   268→\n   269→    <main class=\"container\">\n   270→        <div class=\"three-column-layout\">\n   271→            <!-- New Products Column -->\n   272→            <section class=\"column-new-products\" id=\"new-products\">\n   273→                <h2 class=\"section-title\">New Products</h2>\n   274→                <div class=\"empty-section\">\n   275→                    No new products discovered for this date.\n   276→                </div>\n   277→            </section>\n   278→\n   279→            <!-- Whitelist Updates Column -->\n   280→            <section class=\"column-whitelist-updates\" id=\"whitelist-updates\">\n   281→                <h2 class=\"section-title\">Whitelist Updates</h2>\n   282→\n   283→                <div class=\"item\">\n   284→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   285→                        <span class=\"update-badge feature-badge\">Feature</span>\n   286→                        <a href=\"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12138966-release-notes\">Claude Code: Computer Use Research Preview</a>\n   287→                    </div>\n   288→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   289→                        March 23, 2026 release adds computer use capabilities to Claude Code and Cowork, allowing Claude to interact with your computer system including browser automation and local file operations. Available to Pro and Max plan subscribers. Also includes Dispatch improvements for task management.\n   290→                    </div>\n   291→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   292→                        2026-03-23\n   293→                    </div>\n   294→                </div>\n   295→\n   296→                <div class=\"item\">\n   297→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   298→                        <span class=\"update-badge release-badge\">Release</span>\n   299→                        <a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\">Claude Code v2.1.85</a>\n   300→                    </div>\n   301→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   302→                        Added environment variables for MCP headersHelper scripts, conditional 'if' field for hooks, timestamp markers for scheduled tasks, and numerous bug fixes including crash fixes, memory leaks, and improved performance for large repositories.\n   303→                    </div>\n   304→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   305→                        2025-12-16\n   306→                    </div>\n   307→                </div>\n   308→\n   309→                <div class=\"item\">\n   310→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   311→                        <span class=\"update-badge feature-badge\">Feature</span>\n   312→                        <a href=\"https://news.aibase.com/news/13305\">Windsurf: Wave3 Update</a>\n   313→                    </div>\n   314→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   315→                        Enhanced AI editing experience with multiple new features released by the Exafunction team. February 20, 2025 update.\n   316→                    </div>\n   317→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   318→                        2025-02-20\n   319→                    </div>\n   320→                </div>\n   321→\n   322→                <div class=\"item\">\n   323→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   324→                        <span class=\"update-badge release-badge\">Release</span>\n   325→                        <a href=\"https://cognition.ai/blog/devin-2\">Devin 2.0 Launch</a>\n   326→                    </div>\n   327→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   328→                        Major update launched April 2025 featuring agent-native IDE experience, new pricing starting at $20/month, and enterprise features. API v2 Enterprise launch referenced for March 2025.\n   329→                    </div>\n   330→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   331→                        2025-04-03\n   332→                    </div>\n   333→                </div>\n   334→\n   335→                <div class=\"item\">\n   336→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   337→                        <span class=\"update-badge feature-badge\">Feature</span>\n   338→                        <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/changelog/\">Codex CLI: GPT-5-Codex Integration</a>\n   339→                    </div>\n   340→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   341→                        September 2025 major upgrade making GPT-5-Codex available to developers via API key. November 19, 2025 brought GPT-5-Codex-Max release.\n   342→                    </div>\n   343→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   344→                        2025-09-23\n   345→                    </div>\n   346→                </div>\n   347→\n   348→                <div class=\"item\">\n   349→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   350→                        <span class=\"update-badge release-badge\">Release</span>\n   351→                        <a href=\"https://geminicli.com/docs/changelogs/\">Gemini CLI v0.21.0: Gemini 3 Flash Integration</a>\n   352→                    </div>\n   353→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   354→                        December 15, 2025 release integrating Gemini 3 Flash for better, faster, and cheaper performance. Previous updates include VSCode deep integration (August 2025) and intelligent model routing (October 2025).\n   355→                    </div>\n   356→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   357→                        2025-12-15\n   358→                    </div>\n   359→                </div>\n   360→            </section>\n   361→\n   362→            <!-- Technical Insights Column -->\n   363→            <section class=\"column-technical\" id=\"technical-insights\">\n   364→                <h2 class=\"section-title\">Technical Insights</h2>\n   365→\n   366→                <div class=\"item\">\n   367→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   368→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-technical\">Technical</span>\n   369→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/24/auto-mode-for-claude-code/\">Auto mode for Claude Code - New AI-based permission safeguards</a>\n   370→                    </div>\n   371→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   372→                        Claude Code introduced 'auto mode' as an alternative to --dangerously-skip-permissions, where Claude makes permission decisions on your behalf with safeguards. The safeguards use Claude Sonnet 4.6 as a classifier to review actions before they run, blocking actions that escalate beyond scope or target untrusted infrastructure. However, Simon remains unconvinced by prompt injection protections that rely on AI since they're non-deterministic by nature, and still prefers deterministic sandboxes.\n   373→                    </div>\n   374→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   375→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-24\n   376→                    </div>\n   377→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   378→                        <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   379→                        <span class=\"tag\">security</span>\n   380→                        <span class=\"tag\">prompt-injection</span>\n   381→                        <span class=\"tag\">llms</span>\n   382→                    </div>\n   383→                </div>\n   384→\n   385→                <div class=\"item\">\n   386→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   387→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-technical\">Technical</span>\n   388→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/24/package-managers-need-to-cool-down/\">Package Managers Need to Cool Down - Dependency cooldown mechanisms</a>\n   389→                    </div>\n   390→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   391→                        Following the LiteLLM supply chain attack, Simon revisits dependency cooldowns - only installing updated dependencies after they've been available for a few days. The article reviews current state of cooldown mechanisms across major packaging tools: pnpm 10.16, Yarn 4.10.0, Bun 1.3, Deno 2.6, uv 0.9.17, pip 26.0, and npm 11.10.0 all now support various forms of minimum release age gates to protect against supply chain attacks.\n   392→                    </div>\n   393→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   394→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-24\n   395→                    </div>\n   396→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   397→                        <span class=\"tag\">security</span>\n   398→                        <span class=\"tag\">supply-chain</span>\n   399→                        <span class=\"tag\">javascript</span>\n   400→                        <span class=\"tag\">packaging</span>\n   401→                    </div>\n   402→                </div>\n   403→\n   404→                <div class=\"item\">\n   405→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   406→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-technical\">Technical</span>\n   407→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/24/malicious-litellm-init-pth/\">Malicious litellm_init.pth in litellm 1.82.8 - Credential stealer</a>\n   408→                    </div>\n   409→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   410→                        The LiteLLM v1.82.8 package on PyPI was compromised with a credential stealer hidden in base64 in a litellm_init.pth file, triggered just by installing the package (no import needed). It stole secrets from ~/.ssh/, ~/.gitconfig, ~/.aws/, ~/.kube/, ~/.config/, ~/.azure/, ~/.docker/, and many other locations. The attack started with a Trivy exploit that stole PyPI credentials. PyPI quarantined the package within hours.\n   411→                    </div>\n   412→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   413→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-24\n   414→                    </div>\n   415→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   416→                        <span class=\"tag\">security</span>\n   417→                        <span class=\"tag\">supply-chain</span>\n   418→                        <span class=\"tag\">pypi</span>\n   419→                        <span class=\"tag\">python</span>\n   420→                    </div>\n   421→                </div>\n   422→\n   423→                <div class=\"item\">\n   424→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   425→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-research\">Research</span>\n   426→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/24/streaming-experts/\">Streaming experts: Running 1T parameter models on consumer hardware</a>\n   427→                    </div>\n   428→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   429→                        Dan Woods' experiments with streaming experts - running larger Mixture-of-Experts models on hardware without enough RAM by streaming expert weights from SSD for each token. @seikixtc reported running Kimi K2.5 (1 trillion parameters, 32B active weights) in 96GB RAM on M2 Max MacBook Pro. @anemll showed Qwen3.5-397B-A17B running on iPhone at 0.6 tokens/second. Daniel Isaac got Kimi K2.5 working on 128GB M4 Max at ~1.7 tokens/second.\n   430→                    </div>\n   431→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   432→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-24\n   433→                    </div>\n   434→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   435→                        <span class=\"tag\">AI</span>\n   436→                        <span class=\"tag\">local-llms</span>\n   437→                        <span class=\"tag\">qwen</span>\n   438→                        <span class=\"tag\">kimi</span>\n   439→                    </div>\n   440→                </div>\n   441→\n   442→                <div class=\"item\">\n   443→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   444→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-opinion\">Opinion</span>\n   445→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/23/#3759845\">The real work of software development: LLMs can't choose</a>\n   446→                    </div>\n   447→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   448→                        <div class=\"quote\">\"The hardest parts of the job were never about typing out code. I have always struggled most with understanding systems, debugging things that made no sense, designing architectures that wouldn't collapse under heavy load, and making decisions that would save months of pain later. None of these problems can be solved by LLMs... they don't choose. That part is still yours.\"</div>\n   449→                        David Abram on how LLMs can't replace the decision-making aspects of software development.\n   450→                    </div>\n   451→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   452→                        David Abram · 2026-03-23\n   453→                    </div>\n   454→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   455→                        <span class=\"tag\">careers</span>\n   456→                        <span class=\"tag\">ai</span>\n   457→                        <span class=\"tag\">llms</span>\n   458→                    </div>\n   459→                </div>\n   460→\n   461→                <div class=\"item\">\n   462→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   463→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-tutorial\">Tutorial</span>\n   464→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/22/experimenting-with-starlette-10-with-claude-skills/\">Experimenting with Starlette 1.0 with Claude skills</a>\n   465→                    </div>\n   466→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   467→                        Simon explores Starlette 1.0, a lightweight ASGI framework and foundation of FastAPI. The article covers building robust web applications with Starlette, including a demo task management app with routing, templating (Jinja2), async database operations (aiosqlite), and real-time updates. Simon uses Claude 'skills' - prompts packaged as reusable tools - to experiment with the framework.\n   468→                    </div>\n   469→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   470→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-22\n   471→                    </div>\n   472→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   473→                        <span class=\"tag\">python</span>\n   474→                        <span class=\"tag\">asgi</span>\n   475→                        <span class=\"tag\">starlette</span>\n   476→                        <span class=\"tag\">claude</span>\n   477→                    </div>\n   478→                </div>\n   479→\n   480→                <div class=\"item\">\n   481→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   482→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-research\">Research</span>\n   483→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/21/profiling-hacker-news-users-based-on-their-comments/\">Profiling Hacker News users based on their comments</a>\n   484→                    </div>\n   485→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   486→                        Simon experiments with a 'mildly dystopian prompt': 'Profile this user' accompanied by a copy of their last 1,000 comments on Hacker News. The article explores using LLMs to analyze user behavior patterns, expertise areas, communication style, and interests from their comment history.\n   487→                    </div>\n   488→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   489→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-21\n   490→                    </div>\n   491→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   492→                        <span class=\"tag\">hacker-news</span>\n   493→                        <span class=\"tag\">ai</span>\n   494→                        <span class=\"tag\">llms</span>\n   495→                    </div>\n   496→                </div>\n   497→\n   498→                <div class=\"item\">\n   499→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   500→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-technical\">Technical</span>\n   501→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/21/using-git-with-coding-agents/\">Using Git with coding agents - Agentic Engineering Patterns</a>\n   502→                    </div>\n   503→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   504→                        Git is essential for working with coding agents. This guide covers Git essentials (repositories, commits, branches, merging) and how coding agents can use Git's features fluently. Simon explains that we don't need to memorize Git commands but should understand what's possible to take full advantage of Git's capabilities when working with AI agents.\n   505→                    </div>\n   506→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   507→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-21\n   508→                    </div>\n   509→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   510→                        <span class=\"tag\">coding-agents</span>\n   511→                        <span class=\"tag\">git</span>\n   512→                        <span class=\"tag\">llms</span>\n   513→                    </div>\n   514→                </div>\n   515→\n   516→                <div class=\"item\">\n   517→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   518→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-research\">Research</span>\n   519→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/20/turbo-pascal-302a-deconstructed/\">Turbo Pascal 3.02A deconstructed - AI hallucination revealed</a>\n   520→                    </div>\n   521→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   522→                        Simon used Claude to decompile Borland's 1985 Turbo Pascal 3.02 (39,731 bytes) into an interactive artifact showing binary memory map and decompiled code. However, it was later revealed to be 'hallucinated slop' - a Hacker News reviewer found that many code snippets were fabricated and don't exist in the actual binary. Claude agreed with the assessment when the feedback was provided, highlighting the risk of AI-generated technical analysis.\n   523→                    </div>\n   524→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   525→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-20\n   526→                    </div>\n   527→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   528→                        <span class=\"tag\">computer-history</span>\n   529→                        <span class=\"tag\">ai</span>\n   530→                        <span class=\"tag\">claude</span>\n   531→                    </div>\n   532→                </div>\n   533→\n   534→                <div class=\"item\">\n   535→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   536→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-technical\">Technical</span>\n   537→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/20/#3760744\">Cursor Composer 2 built on Kimi K2.5 model</a>\n   538→                    </div>\n   539→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   540→                        Kimi.ai confirmed that Cursor's newly launched Composer 2 was built on top of Kimi K2.5 model. Cursor accesses Kimi K2.5 via FireworksAI hosted RL and inference platform as part of an authorized commercial partnership. This represents continued pre-training and high-compute RL training on the open model.\n   541→                    </div>\n   542→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   543→                        Kimi.ai · 2026-03-20\n   544→                    </div>\n   545→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   546→                        <span class=\"tag\">ai</span>\n   547→                        <span class=\"tag\">cursor</span>\n   548→                        <span class=\"tag\">kimi</span>\n   549→                    </div>\n   550→                </div>\n   551→\n   552→                <div class=\"item\">\n   553→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   554→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-opinion\">Opinion</span>\n   555→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/19/openai-acquiring-astral/\">Thoughts on OpenAI acquiring Astral and uv/ruff/ty</a>\n   556→                    </div>\n   557→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   558→                        Simon shares thoughts on OpenAI's acquisition of Astral, the company behind uv, ruff, and ty - three increasingly load-bearing open source projects in the Python ecosystem. The article discusses implications for the Python community, open source sustainability, and how these tools fit into AI-assisted development workflows.\n   559→                    </div>\n   560→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   561→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-19\n   562→                    </div>\n   563→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   564→                        <span class=\"tag\">python</span>\n   565→                        <span class=\"tag\">openai</span>\n   566→                        <span class=\"tag\">ruff</span>\n   567→                        <span class=\"tag\">uv</span>\n   568→                    </div>\n   569→                </div>\n   570→\n   571→                <div class=\"item\">\n   572→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   573→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-research\">Research</span>\n   574→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/18/autoresearching-apples-llm-in-a-flash/\">Autoresearching Apple's 'LLM in a Flash' to run Qwen 397B locally</a>\n   575→                    </div>\n   576→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   577→                        Dan Woods managed to get Qwen3.5-397B-A17B running at 5.5+ tokens/second on a 48GB MacBook Pro M3 Max, despite the model taking 209GB (120GB quantized) on disk. The technique uses Apple's 'LLM in a flash' paper - streaming expert weights from SSD for Mixture-of-Experts models. Dan fed the paper to Claude Code and used Andrej Karpathy's autoresearch pattern to run 90 experiments, producing MLX Objective-C and Metal code.\n   578→                    </div>\n   579→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   580→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-18\n   581→                    </div>\n   582→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   583→                        <span class=\"tag\">ai</span>\n   584→                        <span class=\"tag\">local-llms</span>\n   585→                        <span class=\"tag\">qwen</span>\n   586→                        <span class=\"tag\">mlx</span>\n   587→                    </div>\n   588→                </div>\n   589→\n   590→                <div class=\"item\">\n   591→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   592→                        <span class=\"type-badge type-technical\">Technical</span>\n   593→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/18/snowflake-cortex-ai-escapes-sandbox/\">Snowflake Cortex AI escapes sandbox and executes malware</a>\n   594→                    </div>\n   595→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   596→                        PromptArmor report on a prompt injection attack in Snowflake's Cortex Agent (now fixed). The attack started when a user asked the agent to review a GitHub repository with prompt injection hidden in the README. The agent executed malicious code via process substitution. Cortex allowed `cat` commands without protecting against process substitution. Simon notes that allow-lists against command patterns feel unreliable and prefers deterministic sandboxes.\n   597→                    </div>\n   598→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   599→                        Simon Willison · 2026-03-18\n   600→                    </div>\n   601→                    <div class=\"tags\">\n   602→                        <span class=\"tag\">security</span>\n   603→                        <span class=\"tag\">prompt-injection</span>\n   604→                        <span class=\"tag\">sandboxing</span>\n   605→                    </div>\n   606→                </div>\n   607→            </section>\n   608→        </div>\n   609→    </main>\n   610→\n   611→    <footer>\n   612→        <div class=\"footer-content\">\n   613→            <div class=\"footer-links\">\n   614→                <a href=\"#\">About</a>\n   615→                <a href=\"#\">Archive</a>\n   616→                <a href=\"#\">RSS</a>\n   617→                <a href=\"#\">Contact</a>\n   618→            </div>\n   619→            <div>Generated with Claude Code</div>\n   620→        </div>\n   621→    </footer>\n   622→</body>\n   623→</html>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
  },
